WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.200
Let's all pay our condolences to Cats as it
fades away to the Heaviside Layer.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.660
The film adaptation of the famous Andrew Lloyd
Webber stage musical finally hit theatres

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.880
on December 20th, 2019, and audiences have
been passionately avoiding the film ever since.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.779
Here's what went wrong with Cats.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.320
Well, the box office numbers have rolled in
for director Tom Hooper's misguided movie-musical,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.840
and let it suffice to say, they aren't exactly
the cat's meow.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.260
In fact, they're absolutely abysmal.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.180
Initial box office estimates predicted that
Cats would earn about $15 million on its opening

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:35.700
weekend.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.899
But when the film opened - against Star Wars:
The Rise of Skywalker, no less - it pulled

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.000
in a dismal $6.5 million domestically.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.060
That is to say, the box office returns were
effectively in the litter box.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:48.600
"Cats!

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.700
Barely scratching by.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.200
The star-studded film managed just $6.5 million
dollars."

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:54.720
"Mrrrw!"

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.780
Such numbers would be acceptable for, say,
a limited-release art film.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.840
But it's completely unacceptable for a tentpole
Christmas release packed with triple-A talent

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.120
- particularly since the film cost approximately
$100 million to make.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.360
A poor outcome for Cats was rather expected,
but the severity of its opening weekend failure

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.200
is still rather surprising.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.000
Not to put too fine a point on it, but this
is a serious box office bomb we're talking

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.280
about here… a major cinematic cat-astrophe.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.100
$2.2 million dollars on a wide-release opening
day means that basically no one saw this movie.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.660
Meanwhile, pretty much everyone on the planet
knew the film was happening.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.300
Seems like morbid curiosity just wasn't enough
to get people into those seats.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.220
That lack of curiosity killed Cats.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.790
We can't talk about Cats without talking about
that soul-bending teaser trailer, which was

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.700
released back in July 2019.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.660
What a day that was for social media.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:56.260
They say that "all press is good press," but
they clearly hadn't seen the Cats teaser trailer

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.640
before making that statement.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.420
Many people reacted with abject horror when
they got their first look at the sprightly

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:08.620
CGI cats, recoiling in horror at all the chirpy
celebrity faces grafted onto furry bodies.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.720
Some folks hoped against hope that the film
was still very much a work in progress.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.260
"I'm not sure, are they not done?

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.940
Like some of the shots, the one shot of Jennifer
Hudson right at the ending, it looks really

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:19.440
bad.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.080
I'm wondering, have they not done the visual
effects yet?"

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.920
A film is basically doomed if the trailer
becomes a meme based on how dreadful it is

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.980
- especially if that trailer is released less
than six months before the movie comes out.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:38.200
And people weren't exactly won over when the
second full Cats trailer was released in November

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:38.980
2019.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.210
We imagine they were still trying to process
what they'd witnessed over the summer.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.700
When a film relies heavily on post-production
VFX, it takes a lot of time to get just right.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.420
Rushing the process is a really bad idea,
particularly when the filmmakers are busy

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.380
hailing the special effects as game-changing
and groundbreaking.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.240
Well, it sounds like a very special kind of
technology was used for this film:

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.880
"We've used digital fur technology to create
the most perfect covering of fur."

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.410
"These are people… but they're cats!

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.060
And this is kind-of blowing my mind."

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.120
Pixar movies have an average development cycle
of four to seven years from concept design

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.520
to finished animation.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.900
On the other hand, Cats only had seven months
of post-production to render everything.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.640
Principal photography began back in December
2018 and wrapped in April 2019.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.940
Universal Pictures released the teaser trailer
a mere three months after that, and Cats was

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:40.480
released to theatres in December 2019 - just
a little over a year after principal photography

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:41.100
commenced.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.860
That's an absolutely insane schedule - particularly
for a film that requires so much post-production

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:47.360
work.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.860
Reviews for Cats aren't just disparaging - they're
devastating.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.460
Upon its release, critics pounced upon the
film with sheer sadistic glee, and you can't

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.500
really blame them.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.960
As of the making of this video, Cats' Rotten
Tomatoes score stands at a resolutely sad-making

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.790
18 percent - and the selected pull quotes
are essentially an all-you-can-eat buffet

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:09.660
of brutality.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:15.760
Even Rolling Stone's overly precious reviewer
Peter Travers had his claws out, writing:

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.440
"This all-star, all-awful screen take on the
smash Broadway musical easily scores as the

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.000
worst movie of the year and arguably the decade.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.060
Cats shouldn't happen to a dog."

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:30.460
And Variety's chief film critic Peter Debruge
certainly wasn't any kinder:

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.210
"It is ugly.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.020
It is badly directed.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.400
It is an eyesore.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.630
And these cats look terrible."

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.500
Shortly after Cats was released, the absolutely
unthinkable happened.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.000
As Gizmodo put it:

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.980
"In an unprecedented move, Universal is sending
theaters a patched version of Cats."

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.240
You heard that correctly.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.860
On the same day the film hit theaters, Universal
emailed thousands of theaters and let them

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.470
know that a rush package containing an updated
version of the film would be arriving sometime

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.940
in the next couple of days.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:07.520
"Seems like they scrambled and they rushed
to re-edit the film."

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:12.010
In fact, as of the making of this video, there's
even a viral tweet that shows viewers how

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:18.100
to know whether they're watching Cats 1.0
or what we'll go ahead and call Cats 1.01.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.400
The tweet reads:

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.340
"How do you know if you have the old version?

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.690
Look for Judi Dench’s human hand, wedding
ring and all."

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.660
What a time to be alive.

